!
! Bytes left on the target after the move, in Trebago (ferry_rules.h FR1):
! avail - need - spare, exact; below 0 the plan is that many bytes short.
! ok is 1 when every input is at least 0, else 0, and the answer is
! divided by it: a negative size is a division by zero, refused. Trebago
! answers any size to 64 bits; Sutra holds each to 9(15) bytes, so past
! that the two disagree and the answer is held.
trebago room
  in  avail(n), need(n), spare(n)
  out room(n)
  var ok(n)
  ok(i) = min(1, max(0, avail(i) + 1)) * min(1, max(0, need(i) + 1)) * min(1, max(0, spare(i) + 1))
  room(i) = (avail(i) - need(i) - spare(i)) / ok(i)
end
